      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended with this question. Here's sine x, and sine two x. Picture a bike pedal going round. Its height above the axle is sine x, and if you pedal twice as fast, its height is sine two x. At the origin, sine x has slope one, because cos zero is one. Sine two x leaves the origin twice as steeply, with slope two. Where does that two come from? And what happens when the inside part isn't two x, but something like x squared?"
