      "costUsd": 0.0115875,
      "pronunciationText": "Now a harder inside: y equals sine of x squared. Its waves bunch closer and closer, like a rider pedalling faster and faster, so no single squash makes that curve. If you only differentiated the outside, you'd get cos of x squared. Test it against the real slopes. At x equals a half, both are about point nine seven, a ratio of one. At x equals one, the real slope is about one point zero eight, but cos of one is only point five four. That's a ratio of two. At one and a half, the ratio is three. The missing factor isn't fixed. It's two x, the derivative of the inside part, x squared. That factor is how fast the crank is turning, compared with sine x: one, two, then three times as fast."
    },
